summary:
  The noise floor behind the microphone score is read across the burst instead
  of before it, so every candidate is judged against its own click.

history:
  peak_of returns seconds from the start of the FILE — its own line folds the
  analysis skip into the result. _hearing_score added that skip a second time
  when turning the answer back into a sample index, which put the click 0.4 s
  late and slid the "quiet" window right over the burst. Measured 2026-09-15:
  a click at sample 48000 was read as 67200, and the window [19200, 66240)
  swallowed it. Every microphone still scored something, and the ratios still
  ordered roughly right in a quiet room, which is why it survived a refutation
  round: the comparison test moved both ears by the same amount. It matters in
  a room with a noisy tail, where the better ear loses.

target:
  package/contents/ui/calibrate.py

find:
      click_i = int(hit[0] * rate)

replace:
      click_i = start + int(hit[0] * rate)

expect:
  Killed by tests/test_calibrate.py::test_the_quiet_window_stops_before_the_click.
  Line-neutral.
